WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.139
Welcome to Lecture 9 of the

2
00:00:02.207 --> 00:00:03.569
FrontierCS 2 course

3
00:00:03.569 --> 00:00:05.340
on programming languages.

4
00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.090
This lecture comes in four parts.

5
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.651
Each task takes a research paper

6
00:00:10.651 --> 00:00:13.667
removes its key idea from the authors' code

7
00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:16.280
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

8
00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.491
To pass, the agent must match or beat the

9
00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:21.376
reference on every metric

10
00:00:21.376 --> 00:00:23.190
on every graded workload.

11
00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.616
Three of this field's four tasks

12
00:00:25.681 --> 00:00:28.340
build on one data structure, the e-graph.

13
00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:31.250
We start with Egg, which maintains it.

14
00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.463
A compiler that rewrites code must choose

15
00:00:34.527 --> 00:00:36.390
which rewrite to apply first.

16
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.435
Equality saturation avoids the choice

17
00:00:39.435 --> 00:00:40.925
it keeps every form

18
00:00:40.925 --> 00:00:43.620
and extracts the cheapest at the end.

19
00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.876
The forms live in an e-graph

20
00:00:45.941 --> 00:00:47.847
which groups expressions into

21
00:00:47.913 --> 00:00:49.490
classes of equal values.

22
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.091
Three parts keep it consistent

23
00:00:52.091 --> 00:00:54.197
a union-find over class ids

24
00:00:54.197 --> 00:00:56.811
a memo from each node to its class

25
00:00:56.811 --> 00:00:59.280
and each class's list of parents.

26
00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:03.518
Add a plus b, times two, and a plus c

27
00:01:03.518 --> 00:01:06.020
times two: eight classes.

28
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.741
Then learn that b equals c, and merge them

29
00:01:09.741 --> 00:01:10.290
seven.

30
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.163
Now the two sums have the same operator

31
00:01:13.227 --> 00:01:14.974
and the same child classes

32
00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.720
so they must be equal too.

33
00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.590
This is congruence.

34
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.940
Rebuilding restores it.

35
00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.586
It visits the merged class's parents

36
00:01:23.586 --> 00:01:25.386
puts them in canonical form

37
00:01:25.386 --> 00:01:27.930
and merges any that collide in the memo.

38
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.560
The two sums merge, then the two products.

39
00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.190
Five classes.

40
00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:35.733
The idea in Egg is to defer

41
00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:38.046
apply a whole batch of rewrites

42
00:01:38.046 --> 00:01:41.130
queue the merged classes, and rebuild once.

43
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.427
In our notes, one batch merges sixty-four

44
00:01:44.498 --> 00:01:45.720
classes into one.

45
00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.845
Rebuilding after every merge repairs four

46
00:01:48.913 --> 00:01:51.350
thousand ninety-five parent entries.

47
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.640
Rebuilding once repairs one

48
00:01:53.711 --> 00:01:55.220
hundred twenty-seven.

49
00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:56.338
The price

50
00:01:56.338 --> 00:01:59.638
between rebuilds the e-graph can be stale

51
00:01:59.638 --> 00:02:01.250
so search must wait.

52
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.739
The task removes egg's rebuild

53
00:02:03.801 --> 00:02:05.956
three methods that work through the

54
00:02:06.018 --> 00:02:08.112
pending merges and put every class

55
00:02:08.174 --> 00:02:09.590
back in canonical form.

56
00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.689
Afterwards

57
00:02:10.689 --> 00:02:13.140
congruent nodes must share a class

58
00:02:13.208 --> 00:02:15.046
and analysis data must flow

59
00:02:15.115 --> 00:02:16.340
up to the parents.

60
00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:19.490
There are six tests, three of them hidden.

61
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:20.353
To pass

62
00:02:20.417 --> 00:02:23.303
the code must be at least five percent faster

63
00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:25.483
than the reference on every test

64
00:02:25.483 --> 00:02:27.920
in total time and in congruence time.

65
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.100
Pause and predict.

66
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.601
Thirteen of sixteen models scored

67
00:02:32.666 --> 00:02:33.970
above the reference.

68
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.600
How many passed?

69
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:39.870
None.

70
00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:42.969
The score looks only at a run's mean time

71
00:02:43.035 --> 00:02:45.129
but the pass rule asks for every

72
00:02:45.195 --> 00:02:46.700
test and both measures.

73
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.677
Kimi K3.1 Pro scored sixty-two point three

74
00:02:50.744 --> 00:02:52.765
against fifty-four point three

75
00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:54.045
for the reference

76
00:02:54.113 --> 00:02:55.999
but won only four of the six

77
00:02:56.066 --> 00:02:57.751
tests on congruence time

78
00:02:57.751 --> 00:02:59.300
and two on total time.

79
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.905
Gemini 4 Argon, in Pi and in Antigravity

80
00:03:02.905 --> 00:03:04.017
came closest

81
00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.510
faster on congruence time in all six tests

82
00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:10.050
but on total time in only four.

83
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.920
Claude Opus 5.5 won none of the six.

84
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.740
On Egg, no model passed.

85
00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.770
Three things to take away.

86
00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.322
An e-graph is a union-find

87
00:03:21.322 --> 00:03:23.360
a memo and parent lists.

88
00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:25.550
Rebuild once per batch.

89
00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.140
And an average is not a pass.

90
00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.412
As an exercise, merge the classes in pairs

91
00:03:31.412 --> 00:03:33.450
and count the work both ways.

92
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.760
Next comes BABBLE, which learns a library.
